ust be so emotional for you as a  host  it's just hard i'm still figuring it out  for myself every night you know when we  would  rap shooting i would  be filled with so many thoughts and  ideas because it's such a  emotional journey not only for  our subjects but for us as well you know  i definitely see the world  even more brightly now a lot of people  think that  fashion is vain how has fashion inspired  you throughout your own journey in my  journey as a trans person as a trans man  i started  when i was around 32 and i came out and  because i was a singer  i was told that i wasn't going to be  able to go on testosterone at the  beginning  because i might lose my voice and the  only tool  that i had for five years to express my  my gender and the way i felt internally  was through fashion  so for me i will actually say that it  wasn't vanity  in the least it was literally  life-saving for me and i think for queer  folks  specifically it is such a way to tell  the world  who we are and to sort of communicate  that message  to people so that they sort of walk in  with a little bit of who we are already  when when we walk into the situation my  identity as a trans man has changed  i call myself trans bodied now and i  call myself a man  mostly i just call myself lucas lucas  you were the first out  trans man signed to a major record deal  which is  history making that is history right  there every time i hear that being said  that i was the first i would trans man  sign to a record label deal it always it  still hits me in this very strange way  because i  i see myself as just like you know just  like a regular guy or whatever  i'm honored that i had the capacity and  the privilege to be able to do that i  had kids tell me that  they've never seen somebody who was like  them and that it literally had saved  their lives  and that's something that i hold with a  lot of responsibility  and something that i learned coming into  doing this  show as well that there are 